fact WorkItem(id: "WI-365", description: some(value: "Typer facility: abstract spec-op SELF-typing — type a default operation body on an abstract sort, and consume that sort over a provider. Prerequisite for WI-362 (and broadly for WI-324 rule->operation cleanup, which needs abstract-sort operation bodies to typecheck). Surfaced implementing WI-362: giving Stream.collect/takeN a DEFAULT BODY over the primitive splitFirst fails to load with two typer errors, and consuming a List AS a Stream for the abstract ops (head/collect/takeN) fails the same way.\n\nPROBLEM (two linked gaps).\n\n(1) DEFINITION-SIDE (the crux). For `operation collect(s: Stream) -> List[T = T] effects E = match splitFirst(s) case none() -> nil case some(pair(h, rest)) -> cons(head: h, tail: collect(rest))` declared INSIDE the abstract Stream sort: the parameter `s: Stream` is typed Stream[T = ?_, E = ?_] (fresh inference vars), NOT Stream[T = T, E = E] (the enclosing sorts own params). So `splitFirst(s)` -- an ABSTRACT self-receiver spec-op call -- returns Option[Pair[A = ?_, B = Stream]] @ ?_, with the element and the effect both unthreaded. Result: two load errors -- (a) ELEMENT: `type mismatch in match.rule: expected List[T = T], got List[T = ?_]` (h: ?_ from the unthreaded Pair.A, and bare `nil` is List[?_]; the branch-join + return check see List[?_] not List[T]); (b) EFFECT: `type mismatch in collect.effects: expected declared: [E], got undeclared effect: ?_` (splitFirsts row var never unifies with the ops declared E). ROOT: the WI-357 fix bind_spec_params_from_carrier (kb/typing.rs:3093) only fires for a CONCRETE ReceiverCarrier::Concrete (a List[Int] dispatched as a Stream); the ABSTRACT self-receiver path (receiver type == the enclosing spec sort, typing.rs:3075-3076 types through the interface) binds NEITHER the spec ops own type params NOR its effect row to the enclosing sorts params.\n\n(2) CALL-SIDE (companion). `collect([1,2,3])` / `head([1,2,3])` (consume a List as a Stream): head/collect/takeN have no operation BODY on any carrier (head/tail/isEmpty are rule-defined; collect/takeN undefined), so spec-op dispatch finds no carrier impl and raises MissingRequiresForSpecOp -> `spec op called at abstract type -- add requires Stream[T = …, E = …]` (typing.rs:239/250/415), plus the effect cannot ground to {} for a pure List -> `expected [], got undeclared effect: ?_` (typing.rs:10858). NOTE: once (1) lands, Stream.collect HAS a typecheckable default body, so `collect([list])` dispatches to that body (no carrier override) and its inner splitFirst dispatches via the WORKING WI-357 concrete path -- so (1) is the crux and largely subsumes the call-side for the default-bodied ops; the residual is grounding the declared `effects E` to the carriers effect at the consumption boundary (the pure-List {} case; relates to WI-301 effect-set-as-type-arg / the WI-357 effect-close at typing.rs:3115).\n\nDESIGN / FIX DIRECTION. Add the DEF-SIDE DUAL of WI-357: when typechecking an operation body declared inside sort S, a reference whose type is the bare enclosing sort S binds Ss type params to the enclosing sorts OWN param symbols (T -> T, E -> E) -- the self-type of S. Then an abstract self-receiver spec-op call (splitFirst(s)) instantiates the spec op with the enclosing sorts params, threading T and E; the declared `effects E` unifies with the calls now-E effect. Mirror bind_spec_params_from_carrier but source the binding from the ENCLOSING sort identity (env.enclosing_sort, typing.rs:529) instead of a concrete carriers provider fact. The empty-collection branch (`nil`/`[]` : List[?_]) should resolve once the sibling branch is List[T] and the match branch-join unifies ?_ := T (verify against match_branch_join_test; the nil ?_ is likely downstream of the element ?_ and clears once h: T). For the call-side residual, ground the spec ops declared effect row to the providers effect at consumption (pure provider => {}), analogous to the concrete-path effect-close already at typing.rs:3115-3120.\n\nIMPL POINTERS (kb/typing.rs): receiver_carrier / ReceiverCarrier (3077), bind_spec_params_from_carrier (3093) + its effect-close (3115), abstract-receiver interface path (3075), MissingRequiresForSpecOp (239/415), enclosing_sort/set_enclosing_sort (522/529), walk_type_deep_value (3098). scaland has NO typer -> Rust-only.\n\nACCEPTANCE. (a) `operation collect(s: Stream) -> List[T = T] effects E = match splitFirst(s) …` and the analogous takeN typecheck (load) on the abstract Stream sort -- element and effect thread, no spurious requires. (b) `length(collect([1,2,3]))` evaluates to 3 (the WI-362 trip-wire test wi362_collect_over_list_when_typer_grounds_effect_and_element, un-ignored). (c) the WI-365 effect trip-wire `wi365_effect_grounding_test::dispatched_spec_peek_surfaces_modify_effect` (added 2026-06-02; tests/include/wi365_effect_grounding_test.rs; currently `#[ignore]`) passes un-ignored -- a non-pure carrier's `Modify[b]` is DERIVED through the DISPATCHED spec op so a pure consumer is rejected with a diagnostic naming Modify (today the effect row leaks as `?_` + a MissingRequiresForSpecOp and no error names Modify); its companion anchor `direct_carrier_peek_surfaces_modify_effect` (the DIRECT-call dual) already passes today. (d) existing wi357/wi350/wi343/wi325 + full anthill-core stay green. UNBLOCKS WI-362 (its collect/takeN half) and WI-363; RELATED WI-301 (call-side effect-set repr -- closed/Rejected; the effect is derived from the carrier op, not written as a type arg), WI-357 (the concrete-carrier dual, delivered), WI-324 (needs abstract-sort op bodies to typecheck)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-03T06:29:02Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-365", author: "user", content: "PARTIAL delivery (commit 2bdad08, pushed). DEF-SIDE green: Stream.collect/takeN default bodies typecheck on the abstract sort — element threads via types_compatible/has_witness; the effect via STRUCTURAL-IDENTITY comparison in check_operation_bodies (added views_structurally_equal to term_view.rs) replacing the fragile display-name compare that made an abstract sort's row-variable effect E read 'E' (signature) vs '?_' (walked alias Var). PURE-carrier CALL-SIDE effect grounding green: an additive effect-close in check_apply_iter closes the unbound row to {} for a body-ful self-receiver spec op consumed on a pure carrier (was a leaking ?_/undeclared-effect). EVAL green: length(collect([1,2,3]))==3, takeN(..,2)==2 (wi362_*); fixed a latent reduce_var bug — bare nullary constructors none/nil were dispatched as operations (UnknownOperation), surfaced by List.splitFirst's never-eval'd empty case. REMAINING for acceptance (c): the NON-PURE Modify derivation (wi365_effect_grounding_test::dispatched_spec_peek_surfaces_modify_effect, still #[ignore]d) — the effect-close DROPS the unresolved row, correct for a pure List but WRONG for a non-pure Modify[b] carrier which must be DERIVED, not dropped. Also surfaced + filed as a follow-up: consumption-side element precision (collect over a List[Int] takes the caller's expected element via WI-270 seeding, not the carrier). gt needed zero code change (test harness only).", at: "2026-06-02T22:09:10Z")
